  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\mathbb{R}$ denote the set of all real numbers. Let $z_1=1+2 i$ and $z_2=3 i$ be two complex numbers, where $i=\\sqrt{-1}$. \nLet $S=\\left\\{(x, y) \\in \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R}:\\left|x+i y-z_1\\right|=2\\left|x+i y-z_2\\right|\\right\\}$.\nThen which of the following statements is (are) TRUE?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "8R62r",
      "text": "$S$ is a circle with centre $\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{10}{3}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "8wsUV",
      "text": "$S$ is a circle with centre $\\left(\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{8}{3}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "U2F6E",
      "text": "$S \\text { is a circle with radius } \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "fHXYU",
      "text": "$S \\text { is a circle with radius } \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned}\n&\\begin{aligned}\n& |x+i y-1-2 i|=2|x+i y-3 i| \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\quad(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2=4\\left(x^2+(y-3)^2\\right) \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\quad 3 x^2+3 y^2+2 x-20 y+31=0 \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\quad x^2+y^2+\\frac{2 x}{3}-\\frac{20 y}{3}+\\frac{31}{3}=0\n\\end{aligned}\\\\\n&\\therefore \\mathrm{S} \\text { is a circle with centre }\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{10}{3}\\right) \\text { and radius }=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{100}{9}-\\frac{31}{3}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}}=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
